A||||10||Greg Piet||Ocelot cross drilled rotors
Z||000000||Greg Piet||08-29-99||02:32 AM||||The Fiero Srore is selling the crossdrilled rotor package.  I am unhappy with the braking (the stock system I have is in proper order, but not at well as I would like)<br>I was wondering who has tried these and if they are worth the $$$ for street use
Z||000001||batboy||08-29-99||03:30 AM||||I was asking this same question several months ago. Some said cross drilled rotors did very little for street driven cars. Others said it helped in some performance driving conditions and racing. Common sense tells you that the rotor is still a non-vented rotor of the same size diameter and the caliper is the same caliper, you aren't really gaining much if any extra braking with this setup.<p>A number of us here have done the Grand Am/Beretta brake swap. The rotors are thicker and are vented for better cooling, plus the caliper piston is bigger too. Unfortunately, if you do the rear brakes too, you lose your e-brake. My car is an automatic that had a rusted up, nonworking e-brake cable anyway. You can do just the front brakes and then use an adjustable proportioning valve too.<p>For higher performance and/or racing, a number of companies can set you up with some awesome brake systems. Check out Ryane Motor Sports at:<p>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/fiero.htm
Z||000002||Pretender||08-29-99||01:47 PM||||i've got the cross drilled rotors(ocelot) on the front of my '88 and the difference is minamal. but they look damn good!..<IMG SRC="/ubb/redface.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>)
Z||000003||TOM||08-29-99||02:44 PM||||How much are the ocelot drilled rotors? And couldn't I just lay out and drill stock rotors for the same benefit? Sounds like a new product for sale at a way cheaper price.
Z||000004||Greg Piet||08-29-99||03:59 PM||||Well the ocelot pads are suppsoed to be the carbon metallic (i think).  Now most high performance pads (primarily used in NASCAR) work great for their job, but arent worth anything for the general public. <br>I was mainly concerned with this aspect since I wont be driving daytona in my fiero.
Z||000005||Cooter||08-29-99||08:05 PM||||My other car has been plagued with warped brake rotors because the rotors are just too small for the weight of the car. With factory brake pads, the front rotors would need touching up on the lathe every couple of months to keep the car from shaking. I swapped to a carbon-metallic pad and have had no more rotor problems. I cant explain why, but the rotors have held up much better with the carbon-metallic pads. I have put about 30,000 miles on them and they still look great.
Z||000006||theogre||08-29-99||09:11 PM||||Their are a bunch of things that affect braking. Lets make a short list of the most common items. Even tho it's a numbered list the items are all of equal importance.<p>1. Brake pad composition.<br>2. rotor design.<br>3. piston diameter.<br>4. rotor composition.<br>5. front/rear weight and brake balance.<br>6. tire size & design.<br>7. master cyl displacement.<br>8. half a hundred other things.<p>what they mean to you:<br>1. small changes in pad comp often make measurable changes in braking. Pad comps that are more resistant to wasting and overheat will stop better. Heat is obvious, but wasting isn't. Wasting is a combination of gassing and grindoff. Pads that generate excesive waste for an application can actually lift the pads off the rotor.<br>2. rotors that are vented of course dump heat better than solids. The practice of cross drilling or slotting, often done even to vented rotors, is a waste managment tool. Properly drilled rotors are also resitant to warping. Proper drilling requires very high understanding of both brake systems and metalergy.<br>3. An increase in piston ratio increases brake force. This is the main reason for brake improvment in using the "grand am upgrade."<br>4. the material that a rotor is made from, nearly always iron in street cars, desides how venting, drilling, and milling can be used.<p><br>You getting the idea???? there are no simple answers. Yes, the grand am upgrade works in the obvious sense, but at the price of creating legal & insurance issues because it eliminates the e brake. What we need to find there is a rear caliper with bigger bore that Doesn't lose the e-brake.<p>I don't deny that Fiero could use better brakes. I really don't think that many of the "upgrade" options are very well thought out. Fiero Store has a cross drill package that fails to increase pad preasure, a 1-2mm diam change in the caliper pistons would probly be enough. The grand-am option loses the e-brake and causes legal problems. Others cost thousands and aren't worth it.
Z||000007||lowCG||08-29-99||11:53 PM||||I will be looking for some '86 IROC Camaro rear calipers for when I redo my brakes.Not sure if they can be used with the Grand Am rotors or not, maybe after I get my suspension cobbled together I'll have more time to look into it further. 
Z||000008||batboy||08-30-99||05:58 AM||||One time when I was surfing Fiero stuff on the net, I ran across a brake kit that used the 86 Camaro rear calipers (they supposedly have an e-brake lever that can be adapted) and with 4X4 S-10 brakes in front. I believe the rotors on both the Camaro and S-10 are 10.25 inches which are bigger than the Fiero's 9.75 inch ones. The calipers were mounted using special brackets they had engineered and were selling the whole kit for a very expensive price.<p>I did the Grand Am swap on the front first and tried driving that way for a while. Since the front brakes usually do most of the braking, there was a noticeable increase in stopping power, but it needed an adjustable proportioning valve if you were going to leave it that way, which is an option. I went ahead and did the rear too.<p>Now on my daily driver Fiero, I'd want to keep my e-brake, besides it still works but my Batmobile e-brake didn't. Also, like Ogre said, pads make a difference. Just turning the stock Fiero rotors to give you a fresh surface and then using a good semi-metallic brake pad will often make a noticeable change for the better.<p>It's easy to warp a brake rotor if you don't alternate tightening down the lug nuts using a torque wrench when putting a wheel back on. Some of the small Japanese car's rotors will warp if you look at them cross-eyed.
Z||000009||Pretender||08-30-99||12:35 PM||||you can't just make a pretty pattern on the rotors and drill them your self, the cross drilled rotors are made from a harder metal and can withstand more pressure, home drilled rotors usually crack and warp very quickly. 
Z||000010||lowCG||08-30-99||02:40 PM||||Most of the problems with cracking rotors that have been drilled arise from tiny cracks that form around the holes.Unless you take extreme care in the drilling process as far as piloting,speeds and feeds,the cracks are almost guaranteed to be there.Porsche factory cross drilled rotors are actually cast with the holes,so as not to interupt the grain(not unlike wood)structure.<br>Maybe some grooves would be a better bet(?)<br>Pads alone will make a big difference,if you get the right ones(carbon/met).
